<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evaluation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evaluation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Stage 0 measurement artefacts, in the graph rather than beside it.\n\nThis module is not in the roadmap's module list and it earns its place: it is\nwhat lets a prohibited output be a node with edges instead of a paragraph in a\nYAML file. PU-0004 says that attributing the Manual's 'obvious, direct and\nimmediate' formulation to the Act is prohibited and that the error is\nstructurally detectable. It is only structurally detectable if the prohibition,\nthe question it attaches to, and the passages involved are all in the same\ngraph. Here they are, and `shapes/prohibited-use.ttl` reads them."@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evaluation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "TM-Knowledge — evaluation module"@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evaluation> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/provenance>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evaluation> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#versionInfo> "draft — not approved"  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evidence> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evidence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Still almost unpopulated — but for a different reason than the one this\ncomment used to give, and the difference is worth reading.**\n\nUntil 2026-09-12 this module said that populating it *\"is a Part 22/23 job…\nand Part 22 is explicitly out of the pilot scope draft\"*. That sentence was\ntrue when it was written and ADR-0081 falsified it on 2026-09-08: the section\n43 boundary is withdrawn, the whole Manual is in scope, and there is no part of\nthis corpus a module may decline on scope grounds. A boundary that no longer\nexists was still being cited here as a live reason four days later, which is\nwhat a fence left in prose does (ADR-0110).\n\n**The real reason, measured.** 226 chunks across 37 Parts mention evidence, so\nthe corpus has plenty to say. What the *vocabulary* holds is two concepts —\n`GC-0041` and `GC-0101`, both labelled *evidence of use*, and they are one of\nthe ten duplicate-label pairs ADR-0101 put in front of the expert. Two concepts,\none of them a duplicate of the other, is not a taxonomy, and declaring classes\nover it would produce a structure with nothing under it and an expert's name\nnowhere near it.\n\nSo this module stays thin, and its emptiness is now a **gap with a size** —\nthe next concept pass over Parts 22, 23, 28, 34 and the opposition Parts is\nwhat fills it — rather than a boundary. `tmk-ontology-report` counts every\nclass here that holds nothing."@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evidence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "TM-Knowledge — evidence module"@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evidence> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/legal-concepts>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evidence> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#versionInfo> "draft — not approved · unpopulated"  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/examination> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/examination> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Who does what, and to what.\n\n**No longer entirely unpopulated, and the change is worth stating precisely.**\nUntil 2026-09-12 this module said that *\"the approved set describes what the\nManual says about section 43, not a process model of examination, and inventing\none would be authoring practice\"*. The first half is still true of the\n**approved** set and always will be — all 35 signed relationships are sourced\nto `TMM/Part29`. The second half described a prohibition ADR-0079 removed on\n2026-09-08: authoring practice, stamped and evidenced, is the job.\n\nWhat now stands behind these classes is content, not a proposal. The\nvocabulary holds 21 concepts typed `procedural_step` and 8 typed\n`process_role` (ADR-0098), and `authored/relationships.yaml` holds 31\n`isPerformedBy` edges joining the second to the first across the whole Manual\n— acceptance to the examiner, revocation of acceptance to the Registrar,\nopposition to the opponent. **Every one of those was written by a machine and\nread by nobody** (ADR-0109, ADR-0110).\n\nThe role classes were declared empty to make a gap visible. Five of the nine\nterms the reviewer's covering note named\n(`review/returned/260826-expert-feedback.md`, HANDOFF Q17) now have a concept\nrecord behind them and **four still do not** — *Office Practice*, *Subject\nMatter Expert*, *Adverse Report* and *Team Leader* are absent from both stores.\nQ-28's cause is Q-53: a term's definition is usually not in a passage that\ncites anything, so no citation-based rule reaches them, and the boundary that\nwas blamed for this in the first place is gone."@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/examination> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "TM-Knowledge — examination module"@en-AU  .
