<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/relations> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/relations> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The closed list of predicates extraction may propose, in two halves that\nare never added together.\n\n**14 approved terms**, from the 35 relationships in\n`eval/gold/relationships.yaml` that a named expert signed. 6 of them are\nused exactly once, and that number is the one to read before treating this half\nas settled — a term seen once is a term whose boundaries nobody has tested.\nEvery one of those 37 source refs points at `TMM/Part29`, so this half of the\ndictionary is what section 43 needed and nothing else.\n\n**7 authored terms**, from the 219 relationships in\n`authored/relationships.yaml` that a machine wrote across the whole Manual and\nboth instruments. **No expert has read any of them.** 4 of the 7\nappear in no signed record at all and exist only because the corpus outside\nPart 29 states relations the approved half cannot express.\n\nA predicate carries `tmk:ApprovedRelation`, `tmk:AuthoredRelation`, or both.\nReading the class rather than the presence of the term is the difference\nbetween a dictionary an expert built and one a model did (ADR-0109).\n\nDomain and range are `tmk:LegalMatter` throughout. The narrow types each\npredicate has actually been seen with are annotations and infer nothing; see\ntmk:LegalMatter in the legal-concepts module for why."@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/relations> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "TM-Knowledge — relation dictionary"@en-AU  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/relations> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#imports> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/legal-concepts>  .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/relations> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#versionInfo> "draft — generated, not approved"  .
