<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2/prov/0> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "20.5. Grounds for rejection and the presumption of registrability" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "5.5 Section 43 - Trade marks likely to deceive or cause confusion" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "5.5.2" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 20 Definition of a Trade Mark and Presumption of Registrability" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "13"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part20" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "In many instances a ground for rejecting an application under section 43 can be overcome by restricting the specification of goods and services so that the application only covers goods for which the connotation in the trade mark is not deceptive or confusing. Where the trade mark contains or consists of a name or representation which is likely to lead the purchaser to the conclusion that there is a connection between a particular person or organisation and the goods or services bearing the trade mark, evidence that there is such a connection would usually be sufficient to overcome the ground for rejection. Part 29 Trade Marks Likely to Deceive or Cause Confusion" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
