<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/10> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part21" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/10> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "There should be no grounds for rejecting an application for registration of a trade mark that is or contains a shape (that is, a three-dimensional object) merely on the grounds that the applicant has a design registration, or a pending application for registration of a design, for the same three-dimensional object (United States Playing Card Co's Application [1908] 1 Ch 197; Charles Goodall & Son Ltd v John Waddington Ltd (1924) 41 RPC 658; Sobrefina S.A.'s Trade Mark Application [1974] RPC 672 at 680). However, the fact that features of shape and configuration of a container for goods have been registered under the Designs Act, does not mean that they are necessarily adapted to distinguish as a trade mark. (See S.C. Johnson & Son, Inc’s Appn. (1980) 50 AOJP 4200 (Reg)). The usual tests for capacity to distinguish will apply for these applications as for any other." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
