<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/citesCase> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/CASE/2024/FCA/1277> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:81296616514aa5456ce3f7af7e6531ede8fdc11af9adb2c3855af6159f0a8a16" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part21/3/3/2/case/0> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part21/3/3/2/case/1> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part21/3/3/2/case/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "21.3. Shape (three-dimensional) trade marks" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "3.2 Shapes may be capable of distinguishing" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 21 Non-traditional Signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part21" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Applications for shapes as trade marks should be considered under the same broad criteria as any other kind of sign. The appropriate inquiry was set out by Kitto J in Clark Equipment Co v Registrar of Trade Marks [1964] HCA 55 at [5], stating that whether a trade mark is adapted to distinguish should: be tested by reference to the likelihood that other persons, trading in goods of the relevant kind and being actuated only by proper motives - in the exercise, that is to say, of the common right of the public to make honest use of words forming part of the common heritage, for the sake of the signification which they ordinarily possess - will think of the word and want to use it in connexion with similar goods in any manner which would infringe a registered trade mark granted in respect of it. As noted in Kenman Kandy Australia Pty Ltd v Registrar of Trade Marks, [2002] FCAFC 273 (the Kenman Kandy decision), this test applies equally to shapes as it does to words. The ordinary signification is the meaning conveyed by the shape, whether that be entirely generic or functional for the goods and services. In Koninklijke Douwe Egberts BV v Cantarella Bros Pty Ltd [2024] FCA 1277, Wheelahan J said (at [255]): Just as words, such as epithets or words of description or quality, may have functional purposes which render them incapable of being regarded as adapted to distinguish, so also may shapes. The cases show that, as with a word, there must be something “special” or “extra” added by a shape to the goods for the shape to function as a badge of origin: see Koninklijke Philips Electronics NV v Remington Products Australia Pty Ltd [2000] FCA; 100 FCR 90 (Remington) at [16] (Burchett J, Hill J and Branson J agreeing). The Kenman Kandy decision confirms that a shape may be capable of functioning as a trade mark. As such, it is not appropriate to object prima facie that a shape is not functioning as a trade mark merely because it is a shape trade mark and the public will need to be educated to see it as a badge of origin. Effectively the Kenman Kandy decision acknowledged that the shape of the goods could operate as an indicator of trade source, in the same way as words or symbols. As with all trade marks, a shape must be considered in the context of the market in which it is placed, and a decision on its adaptation to distinguish then made using the usual tests." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part21/3/3/2" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
