<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/4~3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/4~3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part21" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/4~3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "In Koninklijke Douwe Egberts BV v Cantarella Bros Pty Ltd [2024] FCA 1277, the shape of a coffee jar was found not to be inherently adapted to distinguish because it was primarily functional, and the features that were not functional drew on the ‘common heritage’ of jar shapes. Therefore other traders were likely to want to use the shape, or shapes nearly resembling it, without improper motive." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/3/3/4~3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part21/3/3/4~3" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
