<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/6/6/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part26" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/6/6/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "As was pointed out in Crook's Trade Mark, (1914) 31 RPC 79 at 85, a trade mark should not be barred from registration because “unusually stupid people, fools or idiots would be deceived”. A similar observation was made in Australian Woollen Mills Ltd v F. S. Walton & Co Ltd, [1937] HCA 51 at 658, where Dixon and McTiernan JJ said The usual manner in which ordinary people behave must be the test of what confusion or deception may be expected. Potential buyers of goods are not to be credited with any high perception or habitual caution. On the other hand, exceptional carelessness or stupidity may be disregarded. The course of business and the way in which the particular class of goods are sold gives, it may be said, the setting, and the habits and observation of men considered in the mass affords the standard." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/6/6/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part26/6/6/9" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
