<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 27 Overcoming Grounds for Rejection under Section 44" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "8"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part27" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "A general letter of consent confirming that one party is prepared to consent to any trade mark applications made by another party may be considered. The general letter of consent must meet the same requirements as a specific letter of consent in relation to the name of the owner providing the consent and to the signatory of the letter (detailed above) and should not contain any conditions (see 3.2.1). It must also identify the applicant to whom the general consent is being given. However, the general consent document need not identify the applicant’s trade mark or the specific goods and services being claimed." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
