<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:f1f98ddc44bccf6601ab233800f69bd8b62edc8c27ccb6bbdfb423460ebf1097" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "2.5" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.2. Scandalous signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "6"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Consideration should be given to context of use, but that alone cannot be the decisive factor. It is appropriate to consider normal, expected usage rather than possible usage. For example, in relation to an application for t-shirts it would not be appropriate to consider how a trade mark would be received when worn in church during a solemn religious occasion as this would not constitute normal expected usage for such goods." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/2/2/5" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/Chunk> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/authorityKind> "practice" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/capturedIn> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/snapshot-c490a9927f1a> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/chunkKind> "body" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/citesProvision> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s42(a)> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:1219e680061857f2bf5232748be9cd25153ded5916ede8b657c581746033e152" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part30/2/2/6/prov/0> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "2.6 Obscene and coarse language in trade marks" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.2. Scandalous signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "While changes in society have resulted in fairly broad acceptance of many scatological and suggestive words, there are some words which in particular contexts are likely to cause offence across a wide section of the community. The risk of offence caused by obscene and coarse language must be determined in the context of the whole application. The word CUNT is still perceived by many as highly offensive and in most cases raising a ground for rejection will be appropriate. The presence of the word FUCK in a trade mark will not routinely give rise to a s42(a) ground for rejection. However, an objection may be appropriate where other circumstances give rise to the necessary level of offence. These factors may include that the goods and services are targeted at sensitive groups such as children, that the mark as a whole is an attack on an individual/group, or otherwise falls foul of other considerations outlined in this part. Whether a word considered by many as offensive language is acceptable as a trade mark will depend to some degree on the amount of invention and imagination used to present it. Phonetic equivalents of strong but commonly used expressions, such as PHAR QUE or FAR KEW, will generally be acceptable if the expression is sufficiently modified by way of humour, thoroughly idiosyncratic spelling (for example, DR PHUQ) or other factors (for example, disguising the word within a device element). Conversely, an overt phonetic equivalent such as FUK U, or C⮍NT where the difference in spelling is minor and it is not softened by anything in the way of humour or subtlety, may still be scandalous and require a ground for rejection, similarly to the word concerned spelt out in full." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/6> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/2/2/6" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/Chunk> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/authorityKind> "practice" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/capturedIn> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/snapshot-c490a9927f1a> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/chunkKind> "body" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:d2c80d3cb57e8c24858b914519f93f651881291f89e31450fffe7b411b619493" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "2.7" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.2. Scandalous signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "8"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Less commonly used expressions, particularly where they also have a double meaning, are far less likely to be offensive to any significant element of society. Calculated concealment of the words, or a clever disguising within a device element may create an acceptable trade mark. Clever advertising campaigns may bring words regarded as coarse into almost acceptable usage. An example is the television campaign that brought the word BUGGER into prominence. There are now a number of registrations containing this word, something that would have been unlikely 20 years ago." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/7> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/2/2/7" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/Chunk> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/authorityKind> "practice" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/capturedIn> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/snapshot-c490a9927f1a> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/chunkKind> "body" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:cf7e0ba7672ed86e713173a9f3a34b380f3e248a91df26f5513615cecb879bc0" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "2.8 Profanity" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.2. Scandalous signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "9"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Profanity is defined in the Macquarie Dictionary in part as meaning characterised by irreverence or contempt for God or sacred things; irreligious, especially speaking or spoken in manifest or implied contempt for sacred things. The Registrar has historically been particularly careful in the handling of trade marks which may have religious connotations. Religion is an issue often surrounded by strong feelings. Particular care needs to be taken with applications incorporating profanity or religious images. Again, the offending words/images need to be obvious within the trade mark, as a ground for rejection is not appropriate if there is only a vague suggestion that something might be profane." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/8> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/2/2/8" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/Chunk> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/authorityKind> "practice" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/capturedIn> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/snapshot-c490a9927f1a> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/chunkKind> "body" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/citesProvision> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s42(a)> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:37c3894d3a8369a7f81db947fd99e6e651c6258f637fdf03782581d664282cd6" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part30/2/2/9/prov/0> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "2.9 Words or images appearing to condone violence, racism and/or terrorism" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.2. Scandalous signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "10"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Post September 2001 the public is more conscious of issues such as terrorism. Trade marks which suggest violence along these lines may cause more public concern than they might have in the past. Trade marks incorporating words and images which appear to condone and/or promote violence, racism or sociopathic behaviours fall within the ambit of scandalous marks. Again the reference needs to be obvious within the trade mark before a ground for rejection can be raised. For example, an application for the word SNUFF for movie studio and/or movie making services in class 41 would attract a ground for rejection under section 42(a), because it is a direct reference to so called \"snuff\" movies, i.e. movies in which people are actually murdered." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/9> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/2/2/9" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/isPartOf> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
