<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3%231> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 32B Examination of Trade Marks for Wines (in Class 33)" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3%231> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3%231> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3%231> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part32B" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3%231> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Trade Marks Act 1995 Trade Mark Regulations 1995 Wine Australia Act 2013 As discussed in Part 22, the Registrar must be satisfied that the trade mark as a whole is capable of distinguishing the goods or services claimed. While wine trade marks are no different in this respect, there are some issues peculiar to these types of trade marks that examiners should be aware of. The appearance of a protected term from the AGWA Register in a trade mark will give rise to careful consideration whether a section 41 ground for rejection applies – particularly if that protected term is the main identifying feature of the trade mark. Some examples are discussed in the following." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part32B/2/3%231> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part32B/2/3#1" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
