<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part47/1/1/2/3~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "9"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part47/1/1/2/3~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part47" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part47/1/1/2/3~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "An opponent can also propose changes to its statement of grounds and particulars at this stage in the process (i.e., before the statement of grounds and particulars is given to the applicant under reg 5.8(2)). This can happen where the opponent believes there is an inadequacy in their statement of grounds and particulars and wants to rectify them even if this office has not raised an issue under reg 5.8(3). In these cases, the delegate can assume that there is an inadequacy. The delegate will not assess the amendments against issues raised under reg 5.8(3) in any previous correspondence. Rather, the delegate will proceed on the basis that there is an inadequacy and assess the amendments in accordance with the requirements of reg 5.8. Any amendment the opponent seeks to make must be allowable under that provision. That is, the amendment, if made, would result in an adequately particularised statement of grounds and particulars." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
