<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/2/2/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part52" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/2/2/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Ex parte hearings may be requested (or set down) under the following circumstances: when, as a result of examination of an application for registration of a trade mark, grounds for rejection are raised under Division 2 of Part 4 of the Act, or the Registrar proposes to reject the application, (outlined in the following Manual parts) Part 22 Section 41 - Capable of Distinguishing Part 26 Section 44 and Regulation 4.15A - Conflict with Other Signs Part 29 Section 43 - Trade Marks Likely to Deceive or Cause Confusion Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law Part 31 Prescribed and Prohibited Signs Part 18 Finalisation of Application for Registration when it is proposed to refuse any application for amendment to the trade mark, the statement of goods or any document filed with the Registrar - Part 6 of the Act, Part 9 Amendments and Changes to Name and Address when it is proposed to revoke acceptance of a trade mark under section 38 3 Part 38 Revocation of Acceptance when it is proposed to refuse an extension of time requested under section 224, Part 15 General Provisions for Extensions of Time when it is proposed to revoke registration of a trade mark under section 84A in any other situation where section 203 mandates a person has the right to be heard. These will arise in a number of contexts, of which refusal to record an assignment is probably the most common. 2 The 1995 Act provides specifically for the setting down of a hearing on the initiative of the Registrar. Generally, the Registrar will propose to reject or refuse the application; the applicant may then request a hearing. 3 A hearing on the matter of revocation of acceptance is not explicitly required by section 203. However, the exercise of this discretion may have serious results for the trade mark applicant and in the interests of procedural fairness the Registrar will give the applicant an opportunity to make representations at a hearing if revocation is proposed." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
