<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "5.3 Review under the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 52 Hearings, Decisions, Reasons and Appeals" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 52.5. Rights of appeal from decisions of the Registrar" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part52" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part52/5/5/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Review of any decision of an administrative nature is available under the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977, (the AD(JR) Act). Therefore, in addition to any specific rights of appeal under the Trade Marks Act 1995 to the Federal Court and the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) or to a review of the decision in the ART, a “person aggrieved” by a decision of the Registrar may seek a review of that decision in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) under the AD(JR) Act. For example, review of a decision of the Registrar to allow an extension of time for filing notice of opposition to registration of a trade mark might be sought by the applicant for registration (see Lyons (trading as Mitty’s Authorized Newsagency) v Registrar of Trade Marks [1983] FCA 236). Review of a decision to refuse such an extension might be sought by a prospective opponent (see Atomic Skifabrik Alois Rohrmoser v Registrar of Trade Marks [1987] FCA 22 and Chiron Corporation v Registrar of Trade Marks [1998] FCA 928). A review under the AD(JR) Act, though conducted in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2), is not a re-examination of the matter. It is not a review of the merits of the decision but goes, briefly and in general terms, to the compliance with procedures under the legislation, provision of procedural fairness and to the availability of evidence on which to base the decision. If the court finds a decision of the Registrar is contrary to law it will not substitute its own decision but may set aside the Registrar’s decision and refer it back to the person who made the decision for reconsideration in accordance with the law (see for example, GS Technology Pty Ltd v Commissioner of Patents [1997] FCA 1460). The court has powers to make orders remedying consequences flowing from an unlawful administrative decision that it proposes to set aside, see British Sky Broadcasting Limited v Registrar of Trade Marks [2002] FCA 1556 at 47. The normal time for applying for review to the Federal Court under the AD(JR) Act is 28 days after receipt of the decision." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
